1
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:04,460
I like your suit.

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:07,180
Oh, thanks. Got a couple new outfits for work.

3
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:08,960
How does it feel knowing your fiancee's job

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,050
is to go out and flirt with doctors,

5
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:11,980
looking like that,

6
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:14,110
while you sit here, you know...

7
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:16,260
looking like this?

8
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,360
She doesn't flirt with doctors.

9
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:20,490
Yeah. It's all very professional.

10
00:00:20,490 --> 00:00:22,690
You know when you bend over, I can see down your shirt?

11
00:00:22,690 --> 00:00:24,220
Okay, good.

12
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:26,910
Speaking of new careers,

13
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:29,160
how are things going with dark matter, Sheldon?

14
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,620
Oh, yeah, I'd have to say it's the most exiting time

15
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:33,200
in the history of the field.

16
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:35,020
What's going on?

17
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:37,450
I started doing it.

18
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:38,990
You know, the government funded

19
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:41,900
the biggest experiment yet to detect dark matter.

20
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:43,240
Oh, yeah, I've read about that--

21
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:46,570
they're sending research teams down into abandoned salt mines.

22
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:49,290
Sheldon, if we apply to be one of those teams,

23
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:51,940
we could be on the ground floor of something big--

24
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,480
not just for theoretical physics but for astrophysics as well.

25
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:57,670
Wait, hang on-- you guys are gonna work in a mine?

26
00:00:57,670 --> 00:00:58,570
Why not?

27
00:00:58,570 --> 00:01:02,060
You had a panic attack when we went through the car wash.

28
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:04,660
Perhaps the emotion you're referring to was shock

29
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,830
at you having something cleaned.

30
00:01:07,970 --> 00:01:09,350
I think what Penny meant is,

31
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:11,920
the thought of you two in a mine is kind of funny--

32
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:14,960
it's like a cat riding a Roomba.

33
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:16,390
If they get scared, they'll have those hats

34
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:18,200
with the lights on them, 'cause down there

35
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,190
it's night-night all the time!

36
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:22,820
Maybe they could ride around

37
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:24,260
in one of those mine carts that go,

38
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:26,480
ee-oo-ee-oo-ee-oo.

39
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:30,950
Yeah, it'll help them get away when they see a gh-gh-ghost...!

40
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,620
- Are they making fun of us? - Yup.

41
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:38,020
I miss the old days when I couldn't tell.

42
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:06,320
So, one of my favorite video games of all time

43
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,940
is called Shadow of the Colossus,

44
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,490
and it was only for PlayStation 2,

45
00:02:09,490 --> 00:02:11,240
but then they came out with a new version

46
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:13,230
for PS3 with better graphics.

47
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:14,510
I finally got the chance to play it,

48
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:18,630
and for some reason, it just wasn't as good as the first one.

49
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:25,240
Or something about yoga.

50
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,420
I'm sorry-- I was thinking about work.

51
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,470
Well, I was just saying that there was this video game...

52
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:33,110
Yeah, that's when I started thinking about work.

53
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,030
Uh, listen, I've got some good news.

54
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:39,610
This is for you.

55
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:45,630
What is this?

56
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:47,440
Well, you know how they gave me the company car?

57
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:50,090
Didn't make sense to have two, so I sold the other one.

58
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:51,460
The one I gave you?

59
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:52,790
Yeah. And there's your money back--

60
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:53,970
and now we're even.

61
00:02:53,970 --> 00:02:55,970
How great is that?

62
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:58,850
Uh, yeah.

63
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:00,590
What?

64
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:02,270
Nothing.

65
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:03,590
Oh, come on, Leonard,

66
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:05,080
I know the car was a thoughtful gesture

67
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,210
and I really appreciate it,

68
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:08,360
but it doesn't make sense to have both.

69
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,580
- You're right. - What should I have done?

70
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:12,450
Taken a picture of us in the car

71
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:13,590
and put it in a pink frame

72
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:18,010
with puffy paint around it that says "Best Fiance Ever"?

73
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:21,590
Doesn't sound that bad.

74
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:24,430
Well, good, because... that's why I did!

75
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,340
Thank you. That-that is so sweet.

76
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:31,540
No, it's not that sweet--

77
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:32,700
I paid for the frame with your money

78
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,750
and then got a massage.

79
00:03:36,130 --> 00:03:38,590
You know what, you don't have to give me this.

80
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:39,710
You keep it.

81
00:03:39,710 --> 00:03:41,330
- Why? - The car was a gift--

82
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:42,220
it was something you needed.

83
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,030
I was happy to help you out.

84
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:46,390
And I really appreciate it, but now I don't need it,

85
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:48,480
so... here you go.

86
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,390
Well, this is silly-- so you don't need the car,

87
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:52,250
but you could use it to buy yourself something else,

88
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:56,000
like a... new purse to put all this money in.

89
00:03:56,610 --> 00:03:57,900
Sweetie, I can buy my own stuff.

90
00:03:57,900 --> 00:03:59,200
I have a good job now.

91
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,320
I know, and I'm proud of you,

92
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:02,860
but that doesn't mean I can't do something nice.

93
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:04,910
Like this.

94
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:07,340
Okay, I don't want this to turn into a fight.

95
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:08,890
- I don't, either. - Great.

96
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:10,340
Thank you.

97
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:15,540
- Can you pass the salt, please? - Sure.

98
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:24,530
In my mind, that broke the tension with comedy

99
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:26,820
and led to sex.

100
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:33,970
Hey, Sheldon, you busy?

101
00:04:33,970 --> 00:04:35,790
I'm always busy.

102
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:39,260
This mind is capable of advanced multitasking.

103
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,230
You know, currently, I'm attempting to solve

104
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:42,770
the Penrose conjecture,

105
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:45,100
I'm composing my Nobel acceptance speech

106
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,640
for when I've solved the Penrose conjecture,

107
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:51,190
and I'm wondering how mermaids have babies.

108
00:04:52,510 --> 00:04:55,250
Don't they lay eggs on a rock?

109
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:58,980
Now I've got room for another thing.

110
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,510
What do you want?

111
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:02,360
So, I did a little research on what

112
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:04,460
the conditions are like in the mines,

113
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:06,060
and the guys might be right--

114
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:07,410
sounds pretty rough down there.

115
00:05:07,410 --> 00:05:09,600
For starters, it's very humid,

116
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:11,380
and about a hundred degrees.

117
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:14,230
Well, I'm from Texas and you're from India--

118
00:05:14,230 --> 00:05:18,770
we're no strangers to the fragrant armpit. Next.

119
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:21,350
It's also a live mine,

120
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:23,580
so there'll be dynamite explosions going off in the distance.

121
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,840
Yeah, I have a lactose-intolerant roommate

122
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:29,060
with a taste for ice cream. Next.

123
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:32,180
Oh, you have to be down there for 12 hours at a time.

124
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:34,510
Yeah? We have to be somewhere.

125
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,100
Well, there's no toilets--

126
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:37,890
we'll have to do our business in a bucket.

127
00:05:37,890 --> 00:05:41,130
So it's settled-- we're not doing it.

128
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,050
Look, I get it, but...

129
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:46,560
before we pass up on an incredible opportunity,

130
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:49,860
I was thinking about when Howard was training to go to space,

131
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:52,090
they put him in a simulated environment.

132
00:05:52,090 --> 00:05:53,700
Interesting.

133
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:55,740
You're suggesting that we recreate

134
00:05:55,740 --> 00:05:57,260
the conditions of the mine

135
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:59,270
- to see if we can handle it. - Exactly.

136
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:01,610
- Very well. - So where should we do it?

137
00:06:01,610 --> 00:06:05,040
Well, I'll just Google "Hot, dark and moist,"

138
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,710
see what comes up.

139
00:06:09,010 --> 00:06:09,860
Uh, Sheldon...

140
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:13,310
Oh, there, well, look, there's all kinds of videos.

141
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:18,010
Hey, you got a sec?

142
00:06:18,010 --> 00:06:19,140
Hi. Sure.

143
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:21,270
I was hoping we could talk about the money again.

144
00:06:21,270 --> 00:06:23,770
- Oh, sweetie, just forget about it. - No, no, no, no,

145
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:26,150
hear me out. I know things got a little weird last night.

146
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:28,060
Well, leaving an envelope full of cash

147
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:29,730
on my dresser after sex

148
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:31,880
would count as weird.

149
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:36,230
Well, I think I came up with a pretty good solution.

150
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,850
Why don't we just put the money in a joint account?

151
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:40,110
It'll be our first money together.

152
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:42,680
We can use it for the wedding, the honeymoon, whatever.

153
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:44,670
You know what, that's great. I love that!

154
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:45,860
Yeah, of course you do--

155
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:48,260
I'm not just a genius in bed.

156
00:06:56,150 --> 00:06:58,310
You sure are, baby.

157
00:07:00,490 --> 00:07:02,550
You two want to be alone?

158
00:07:03,110 --> 00:07:05,250
I want you to know I get why you don't want the money--

159
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:06,940
you've got this new job, you're excited

160
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:08,590
about being financially independent--

161
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:10,650
and I am nothing but proud of you.

162
00:07:10,650 --> 00:07:12,410
Oh, thank you so much.

163
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:14,420
It feels so good to hear you say that.

164
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:15,370
And I want you to know

165
00:07:15,370 --> 00:07:17,070
that I get this job kind of changes

166
00:07:17,070 --> 00:07:18,650
the balance in our relationship,

167
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:21,340
and... I'm trying to be sensitive to that.

168
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:25,920
What?

169
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:28,370
Well, I just think that maybe you're a little hung up

170
00:07:28,370 --> 00:07:30,430
on the money because I'm less reliant on you now,

171
00:07:30,430 --> 00:07:32,440
and that's a little scary.

172
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,550
Maybe.

173
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:41,830
Or maybe I tried to do something nice

174
00:07:41,830 --> 00:07:43,800
and maybe you had a problem with it

175
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,220
because of your control issues.

176
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:50,310
Control issues?

177
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:52,290
Maybe.

178
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,340
Or... maybe...

179
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,680
now that I'm no longer an out-of-work actress

180
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:59,340
who can't pay for her own dinner,

181
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:01,670
that makes you a little insecure.

182
00:08:01,670 --> 00:08:02,810
Well, I can't believe you'd say that.

183
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:06,610
You know how insecure I am about my insecurities.

184
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:09,920
You know what? I'm sorry.

185
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:11,420
It's stupid to fight over money.

186
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:12,650
Yeah, I'm sorry, too.

187
00:08:12,650 --> 00:08:15,210
We're about to share the rest of our lives together.

188
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:17,430
It's our money-- who cares who has it?

189
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:19,630
Ugh! You're right-- who cares?

190
00:08:21,750 --> 00:08:23,080
Get that money out of my back pocket

191
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:25,300
or I will break your fingers.

192
00:08:28,690 --> 00:08:29,620
All this time I never knew

193
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:31,290
there were steam tunnels down here.

194
00:08:31,290 --> 00:08:33,070
Most universities have them.

195
00:08:33,070 --> 00:08:36,010
When I was an undergrad, I spent three days in one

196
00:08:36,010 --> 00:08:38,050
pledging a sorority.

197
00:08:39,510 --> 00:08:40,330
Did you get in?

198
00:08:40,330 --> 00:08:41,600
No. They forgot I was there.

199
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:44,370
But it really opened up my pores.

200
00:08:45,530 --> 00:08:47,410
Well, according to my research,

201
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:50,470
the steam tunnels below this access point

202
00:08:50,470 --> 00:08:53,270
should be the closest analog to the mines.

203
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:54,570
This is gonna be so much more accurate

204
00:08:54,570 --> 00:08:55,950
than the steam room at the gym.

205
00:08:55,950 --> 00:08:57,150
Plus, there's almost no chance

206
00:08:57,150 --> 00:09:00,210
we'll see any of our coworkers half naked.

207
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:02,310
Or totally naked.

208
00:09:02,310 --> 00:09:03,050
I love Howard,

209
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:05,570
but the dude needs a little shame.

210
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,390
All right, Amy, this walkie-talkie is yours.

211
00:09:08,390 --> 00:09:11,080
If we run into any problems, I'll contact you.

212
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:13,530
And if anything bad happens, what's the rule?

213
00:09:13,530 --> 00:09:17,340
Save you first; come back for Raj only if there's time.

214
00:09:17,830 --> 00:09:19,850
We're going to be fine.

215
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:21,670
Sheldon, I'm really impressed

216
00:09:21,670 --> 00:09:23,230
you're willing to try this.

217
00:09:23,230 --> 00:09:26,430
Well, admittedly, this brushes up against my well-known aversions

218
00:09:26,430 --> 00:09:28,560
to heat, small places,

219
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,070
going below floor-level,

220
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:32,560
dampness, hatches, ladders,

221
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:34,420
darkness, echoes,

222
00:09:34,420 --> 00:09:37,670
and eliminating in Home Depot buckets.

223
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:40,320
That last one is quite new, but I have a feeling

224
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:43,190
that's gonna rocket to the top of the list.

225
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:55,090
I'm sweating already.

226
00:09:55,810 --> 00:10:00,020
Yeah, as the person beneath you, allow me to say, I know.

227
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:05,380
How hot is it?

228
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:08,460
Uh, let's see. 704?

229
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:10,780
No, wait, it's on "Clock."

230
00:10:11,410 --> 00:10:13,130
No, the real answer isn't much better.

231
00:10:13,130 --> 00:10:15,800
102 degrees.

232
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:16,870
Well, that's what we wanted.

233
00:10:16,870 --> 00:10:20,110
- This is as hot as the mines will be. - True.

234
00:10:20,670 --> 00:10:25,080
Sheldon's Mine Simulation Log, entry one.

235
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,290
Koothrappali's restating of the obvious

236
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:29,950
is already getting on my nerves.

237
00:10:32,830 --> 00:10:34,240
Thanks again for having us.

238
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:36,840
Oh, of course. What did you guys want to talk about?

239
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:38,870
Well, you know, now that we're engaged,

240
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:39,980
we had some questions about

241
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:42,750
how you handle money as a couple.

242
00:10:42,750 --> 00:10:46,350
I told you they weren't gonna ask us to swing with them.

243
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,440
I didn't think they were going to.

244
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:50,940
I just wanted to have a way to say no

245
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:53,570
without hurting Leonard's feelings.

246
00:10:54,050 --> 00:10:56,700
Well... nailed it.

247
00:10:57,170 --> 00:10:58,540
So what did you want to know?

248
00:10:58,540 --> 00:11:01,260
Well, do you guys ever fight over money?

249
00:11:01,260 --> 00:11:02,990
Sure, sometimes.

250
00:11:02,990 --> 00:11:04,250
I mean, it can be a little awkward

251
00:11:04,250 --> 00:11:07,000
since I make so much more money than Howie.

252
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:10,540
You didn't have to say "so much more."

253
00:11:10,540 --> 00:11:12,350
Well, I didn't have to, but for the sake of accuracy

254
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:14,630
I felt that I should.

255
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:18,350
I brought a lot of significant assets into the relationship, too.

256
00:11:18,350 --> 00:11:19,080
Like what?

257
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:22,760
Your incomplete set of Star Trek collector plates?

258
00:11:23,580 --> 00:11:24,900
For your information, I just bought the last one

259
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:27,060
I needed on eBay.

260
00:11:27,550 --> 00:11:29,850
Without asking me?

261
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:32,290
There were only three minutes left in the auction

262
00:11:32,290 --> 00:11:34,210
and it was a mint condition Scotty

263
00:11:34,210 --> 00:11:36,640
from a smoke-free home.

264
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:39,570
How much, Howie?

265
00:11:39,570 --> 00:11:40,690
Not a lot.

266
00:11:40,690 --> 00:11:42,880
How much?

267
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:46,100
Let's not talk about this in front of our friends.

268
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:50,040
Was it more or less than falconry school?

269
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:54,470
For the tenth time, that was a Groupon.

270
00:11:56,590 --> 00:11:59,140
Like I would swing with him.

271
00:12:03,730 --> 00:12:05,290
How you feeling?

272
00:12:05,290 --> 00:12:06,510
Good.

273
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:09,590
Little claustrophobic.

274
00:12:09,590 --> 00:12:10,820
Let's set up the equipment.

275
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:13,250
It'll help take your mind off of it.

276
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:17,220
Miners often sang mining songs to keep their spirits up.

277
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:18,620
Do you know any mining songs?

278
00:12:18,620 --> 00:12:20,640
Just the hits.

279
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:25,230
* Where it's dark as a dungeon *

280
00:12:25,230 --> 00:12:27,400
* And it's damp as the dew *

281
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:28,240
That's pretty.

282
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:30,760
* Where the dangers are double *

283
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:33,930
* And the pleasures are few *

284
00:12:33,930 --> 00:12:36,470
* Where the rain never falls *

285
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:39,130
* And the sun never shines *

286
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:41,820
* Yes, it's dark as a dungeon *

287
00:12:41,820 --> 00:12:44,260
* Way down in the mine *

288
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:46,740
It's a little more bleak than I thought.

289
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:49,050
* Well, I pray when I dead *

290
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:51,550
* And the ages shall roll *

291
00:12:51,550 --> 00:12:54,590
* That my body will blacken *

292
00:12:54,590 --> 00:12:56,890
* And turn into coal *

293
00:12:56,890 --> 00:12:59,260
Getting kind of grim.

294
00:13:00,550 --> 00:13:03,160
* Then I'll look from the door *

295
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:05,740
* Of my heavenly home *

296
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:08,260
* And pity the miner *

297
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,680
* That mines my poor bones. *

298
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:18,320
Okay. How 'bout a little Miley Cyrus next?

299
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:19,950
Who's he?

300
00:13:23,550 --> 00:13:25,550
Talk about wasted money.

301
00:13:25,550 --> 00:13:27,390
What about the late fees on our credit card

302
00:13:27,390 --> 00:13:29,220
because somebody didn't pay the bill on time?

303
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:30,460
Well, maybe I would have paid it

304
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:32,900
if I wasn't also doing everything else around here.

305
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:35,510
Oh, you're saying I don't do anything around here.

306
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:39,870
Look at my chore chart.

307
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:43,470
- She made him a chore chart. - I see it.

308
00:13:44,210 --> 00:13:47,390
"Do the dishes." There's a star right there.

309
00:13:47,390 --> 00:13:50,230
That was a pity star. Putting water in the roasting pan

310
00:13:50,230 --> 00:13:53,690
and leaving it in the sink is not doing the dishes.

311
00:13:53,690 --> 00:13:57,770
That pan had to soak and you know it.

312
00:13:58,270 --> 00:13:59,670
Well, don't come crying to me

313
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:01,770
when you don't get your allowance.

314
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:03,480
It's not an allowance.

315
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:04,520
It's a stipend!

316
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:06,460
And we said we weren't gonna call it an allowance

317
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:08,970
in front of my friends.

318
00:14:12,750 --> 00:14:14,600
I usually don't like lemon bars,

319
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:17,600
but th-these are really good.

320
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:21,750
So, as Hannah Montana,

321
00:14:22,390 --> 00:14:24,850
Miley was a world-famous pop star.

322
00:14:24,850 --> 00:14:26,760
But then she would take off her wig and go to school

323
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:27,730
like a normal girl.

324
00:14:27,730 --> 00:14:29,040
Which, I don't have to tell you,

325
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:31,920
at that age, is its own headache.

326
00:14:33,030 --> 00:14:34,390
That's preposterous.

327
00:14:34,390 --> 00:14:37,440
How would she go unrecognized just by wearing a wig?

328
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:39,590
But you're okay with Superman concealing his identity

329
00:14:39,590 --> 00:14:40,880
with a pair of glasses?

330
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,720
He doesn't just put on a pair of glasses.

331
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:44,350
He combs back his curlicue

332
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:46,980
and affects a mild-mannered personality.

333
00:14:48,540 --> 00:14:50,320
You guys doing okay down there?

334
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:53,440
I told you to use the walkie-talkie!

335
00:14:54,490 --> 00:14:56,800
You guys doing okay down there?

336
00:14:58,230 --> 00:15:00,040
Please keep this channel clear for emergencies.

337
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:01,480
Thank you.

338
00:15:02,790 --> 00:15:05,970
I'm going to the vending machine. Do you want anything?

339
00:15:06,220 --> 00:15:07,610
This is a simulation.

340
00:15:07,610 --> 00:15:09,900
We have to survive on the supplies we brought.

341
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:12,540
Okay, just checking.

342
00:15:12,540 --> 00:15:15,230
We should have asked her to get some Funyuns.

343
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:17,240
You're not going to have Funyuns

344
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:19,380
when we're a mile below the surface of the Earth.

345
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:21,900
What if we brought them down with us?

346
00:15:22,810 --> 00:15:25,460
We'll take some Funyuns.

347
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:27,010
Anything else?

348
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:29,280
Some York Peppermint Patties,

349
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:30,900
a couple of Dr. Peppers, and...

350
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:33,860
run to Best Buy and see if they have a portable DVD player

351
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:37,560
and season one of a show called Hannah Montana.

352
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:39,710
Have her get season two.

353
00:15:39,710 --> 00:15:42,410
Season one, it was still finding itself.

354
00:15:44,080 --> 00:15:45,600
Sorry you guys had to see that.

355
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:47,460
- Oh, don't apologize. - Yeah.

356
00:15:47,460 --> 00:15:50,980
It just makes it a lot harder to pretend it never happened.

357
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:53,160
Money's a sensitive subject for Howie

358
00:15:53,160 --> 00:15:54,940
because of the difference in our income.

359
00:15:54,940 --> 00:15:56,170
It really bothers him?

360
00:15:56,170 --> 00:15:57,560
Well, sure. There's still a lot of pressure

361
00:15:57,560 --> 00:15:59,240
on guys to be providers.

362
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:00,890
So even though he's happy for me,

363
00:16:00,890 --> 00:16:02,630
it's just a little tough on him.

364
00:16:02,630 --> 00:16:04,890
- Uh-oh. - What?

365
00:16:04,890 --> 00:16:06,450
Well, if I do well in sales,

366
00:16:06,450 --> 00:16:08,590
I could end up making a lot more money than you.

367
00:16:08,590 --> 00:16:10,400
Not a problem. I grew up with a mother

368
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:12,360
who emasculated my father financially

369
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:15,130
and in every other aspect of his life, so...

370
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:19,340
...really, it's what I think love looks like.

371
00:16:21,180 --> 00:16:23,610
I'll be right back. I'm gonna go check on him.

372
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:27,190
Maybe it's a good thing we came here.

373
00:16:27,190 --> 00:16:29,070
- It's like a lesson in what not to do. - Yeah.

374
00:16:29,070 --> 00:16:31,580
I don't want something dumb like money to come between us.

375
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:33,770
It won't. Let's just promise to figure this kind of stuff out

376
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:35,300
before we get married.

377
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:37,360
Why are you being so bossy?!

378
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:39,090
Why are you being a baby?

379
00:16:39,090 --> 00:16:41,830
I'm not a baby! I'm a grown man,

380
00:16:41,830 --> 00:16:45,240
and I made the bed. Now where's my star?

381
00:16:48,050 --> 00:16:49,770
- Should we go? - Hang on.

382
00:16:49,770 --> 00:16:52,400
I want to see if he gets the star.

383
00:16:55,410 --> 00:16:57,550
Oh, this heat is brutal.

384
00:16:57,550 --> 00:17:00,290
As someone from the tropical subcontinent of India,

385
00:17:00,290 --> 00:17:03,020
you should know that fanning yourself in a humid environment

386
00:17:03,020 --> 00:17:05,020
only raises your body temperature.

387
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:08,010
That does explain why the servants used to look so hot

388
00:17:08,010 --> 00:17:10,150
while they were fanning me.

389
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:13,670
Let's get our minds off how uncomfortable we are.

390
00:17:13,670 --> 00:17:16,190
We could take some more simulated instrument readings.

391
00:17:16,190 --> 00:17:17,620
My major focus at the moment

392
00:17:17,620 --> 00:17:20,020
is keeping my claustrophobia at bay.

393
00:17:20,020 --> 00:17:22,200
- How's that going? - You tell me.

394
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:23,580
I feel like I can't breathe,

395
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:25,990
and I am tempted to crack you open

396
00:17:25,990 --> 00:17:28,660
and suck the air right out of your lungs.

397
00:17:30,280 --> 00:17:32,740
Sheldon, if this is too much, we can stop.

398
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:34,060
We're not stopping.

399
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:35,770
You don't have to bite my head off.

400
00:17:35,770 --> 00:17:37,440
I apologize.

401
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:38,460
I j...

402
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:41,410
I'm plagued by an internal struggle.

403
00:17:41,410 --> 00:17:43,120
If you would just use the bucket,

404
00:17:43,120 --> 00:17:45,290
you'd be so much more comfortable.

405
00:17:46,590 --> 00:17:48,870
My struggle is emotional.

406
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:50,680
Is it Amy?

407
00:17:51,030 --> 00:17:52,960
It's dark matter.

408
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:57,670
When I entered the field of string theory, I was a prodigy.

409
00:17:57,670 --> 00:18:01,130
I rose to a position of respect,

410
00:18:01,130 --> 00:18:04,000
and I assumed my career would continue

411
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,520
on that upward trajectory.

412
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:09,040
Now here I am in my 30s,

413
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:11,900
I'm back at square one.

414
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:15,700
And, frankly, it's frightening.

415
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:19,660
Sheldon, you know what I think of when I'm scared?

416
00:18:19,660 --> 00:18:21,760
Voyager.

417
00:18:22,230 --> 00:18:23,480
Voyager the space probe

418
00:18:23,480 --> 00:18:27,020
or Voyager the Star Trek TV show?

419
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:28,580
The space probe.

420
00:18:28,580 --> 00:18:30,210
Good. Because I am too hot and tired

421
00:18:30,210 --> 00:18:33,870
to go on about how much I hate Voyager the TV show.

422
00:18:35,110 --> 00:18:36,580
By the time I was born,

423
00:18:36,580 --> 00:18:39,130
Voyager 1's mission was supposed to be over.

424
00:18:39,130 --> 00:18:40,830
It had seen Jupiter

425
00:18:40,830 --> 00:18:43,040
and Saturn and all their moons,

426
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:45,990
but it kept going.

427
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:48,320
When I left India for America,

428
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:51,120
I was never more scared in my life.

429
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:53,950
I had no idea what lay ahead.

430
00:18:53,950 --> 00:18:56,000
Whenever I feel that way, I think about how...

431
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,130
Voyager is still out there

432
00:19:00,130 --> 00:19:02,570
somewhere beyond our solar system,

433
00:19:02,570 --> 00:19:05,830
going further than anyone ever thought it could.

434
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,650
Don't leave! You can do this!

435
00:19:13,820 --> 00:19:15,260
Sheldon, is everything okay?

436
00:19:15,260 --> 00:19:16,810
It's too late for Koothrappali.

437
00:19:16,810 --> 00:19:18,210
Let's go!

438
00:19:21,170 --> 00:19:24,340
11 minutes. That's longer than I thought.

439
00:19:26,140 --> 00:19:27,100
They're in my shirt!

440
00:19:27,100 --> 00:19:29,510
They're in my shirt! They're in my shirt!

441
00:19:33,650 --> 00:19:35,220
Okay, this is definitely the most fun thing

442
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:36,970
we can do with the money.

443
00:19:40,890 --> 00:19:44,390
I've never done it on a big old pile of cash before.

444
00:19:47,330 --> 00:19:50,360
"Me neither, Leonard. It's my first time, too."

445
00:19:54,650 --> 00:19:57,440
I'm so sorry I made you feel bad about the money stuff.

446
00:19:57,440 --> 00:20:00,340
- It's okay. - No, it's not okay.

447
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:03,010
I want us to be partners, equals.

448
00:20:03,010 --> 00:20:05,580
Adults in a mature relationship.

449
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:07,370
I want that, too.

450
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:11,130
Now, let's talk about your birthday party.

451
00:20:11,130 --> 00:20:12,960
Ooh, laser tag, laser tag. Oh!

452
00:20:16,030 --> 00:20:18,020
Do you really need me to transcribe this?

453
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:19,580
You're not doing it for me.

454
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:21,640
You're doing it for future generations

455
00:20:21,640 --> 00:20:24,200
who will benefit from my struggle.

456
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:28,270
Sheldon's Mine Simulation Log,

457
00:20:28,770 --> 00:20:30,450
entry four:

458
00:20:30,450 --> 00:20:33,070
my Kit Kat has melted.

459
00:20:34,730 --> 00:20:36,680
All is lost.

460
00:20:38,100 --> 00:20:40,430
You call yourself a friend?!

461
00:20:40,430 --> 00:20:42,060
I was trying to help you!

462
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:43,810
And at the first sign of trouble

463
00:20:43,810 --> 00:20:49,000
you ran away, leaving me to fend off a family of rats!

464
00:20:49,650 --> 00:20:51,420
You're a completely selfish human being

465
00:20:51,420 --> 00:20:54,800
and a... and a physical and a moral coward!

466
00:20:56,580 --> 00:21:00,150
His statements of the obvious continue to annoy.

